STRUCTURE OF THE ratings.csv FILE

Four columns, one row per rating: "user userId gave rating rating to movie movieId at time timestamp".
The ratings come from MovieLens (a different website from TMDB).

    userId,	movieId,	rating,		timestamp
    1,		110,		1.0,		1425941529

- userId
	Identifier of the MovieLens user (anonymous: there is no other information about the users)

- movieId
	Identifier of the movie on MovieLens: it is linked to the movies through links.csv
	(ratings.movieId -> links.movieId -> links.tmdbId -> movies_metadata.id)

- rating
	Rating from 0.5 to 5 (a different scale from movies_metadata's vote_average, which goes from 0 to 10)

- timestamp
	Moment of the rating, in seconds since January 1st 1970 (Unix format): 1425941529 = March 9th 2015


FINDINGS FROM THE EDA SCRIPTS


- Structure (section 1):
 No missing values.


- Values (section 2):
	rating has only the 10 expected values (0.5 - 1 - ... - 5), no out-of-scale values.
	Most frequent rating 4 (26.89%), then 3 (20.20%) and 5 (14.65%). Average 3.528.
	Ratings from January 9th 1995 to August 4th 2017.

- Duplicates (section 3):
	no identical rows and no user who rated the same movie twice.

- Users and movies (section 4):
	270,896 users (ids from 1 to 270,896, without gaps), 45,115 rated movies.

	                    min      median    mean    max       only 1 rating      fewer than 10
	ratings per user    1        30        96.1    18,276    5,084 (1.88%)      36,993 (13.66%)
	ratings per movie   1        8         576.8   91,921    7,659 (16.98%)     23,856 (52.88%)

	-> more than half of the movies have fewer than 10 ratings: the average rating of these movies is not reliable
	   (same as vote_average in movies_metadata). Rankings need a minimum number of ratings.

	One user (45811) gave 18,276 ratings, twice as many as the second (9,279).
	196 users with at least 20 ratings always gave the same rating (156 of them always 5).
	They are not errors, but anomalous behaviours that can weigh on the averages.


