Logical checks

- Half stars (section 5):
	up to 2002 no rating has a half star (0.00% every year); the first rating with a half star
	is from February 18th 2003, and from 2004 they are about 40-44% of the ratings.
	So half stars were introduced on MovieLens in 2003: older ratings
	are not "less precise" by the users' choice, it simply was not possible.

- Ratings given before the release of the movie (section 6):
	9,834 ratings (0.04%) have a date earlier than the release_date of the linked movie.

	days before release         ratings
	0-7                         31.64%
	8-30                        19.49%
	31-365                      33.31%
	more than 365               15.56%

	Most are a few days or months: consistent with previews or festivals before the official release.
	47 movies were rated more than one year before release (1,530 ratings).
	Of these, 12 have a different IMDb code in links.csv and in movies_metadata, and collect 1,090 of those 1,530 ratings.
	e.g. movieId 2894 rated since 1999 but linked to "Romance", released in 2012 (different IMDb code).
	-> confirms that those movieIds are linked to the WRONG movie in links.csv (see EDA_links):
	   the ratings are for a movie from the '90s, the link leads to a more recent movie with the same title.
	The other cases (same IMDb code) are highly anticipated movies rated before release,
	e.g. Pirates of the Caribbean: Dead Men Tell No Tales and Thor: Ragnarok.

- Ratings that can be linked to a movie (section 7):

	movieId without tmdbId                          13,503    0.05%
	tmdbId not present in movies_metadata           29,219    0.11%
	wrong movieId on a shared tmdbId                 7,060    0.03%
	linkable                                    25,974,507   99.81%


- ratings_small compared to ratings (section 8):
	no row of ratings_small is identical to a row of ratings,
	but 99.67% of the rows of ratings_small are found in ratings with the same movie, same rating and
	same instant (to the second), only the userId changes.
	For 669 users out of 671, a single user of ratings has at least 90% of their ratings.
	-> ratings_small contains (almost) the same ratings as ratings, with the users renumbered:
	   user 1 of ratings_small corresponds to user 130461 of ratings.
	   So the same userId indicates different people in the two files: they must never be mixed.
	ratings_small goes up to October 16th 2016, ratings up to August 4th 2017.


What emerges
- The file is structurally clean: no missing values, no duplicates, values in the right scale.
- The anomalies are in the links with the other files (links and movies_metadata), not in the ratings:
  99.81% of the ratings are correctly linked to a movie.
- The ratings are very concentrated: half of the movies have fewer than 10 ratings, while one movie reaches 91,921.
